          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong lambda calculus
            is:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a simple
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            %li
              %p
                a
                %em model of computation
                (akin to
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Turing+machine"} Turing machine
                = succeed "s)," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursive+function"} recursive function
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                and predicates; and
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+categories"} cartesian closed categories
                (for more on this see at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %p
            It comes in both
            %em typed
            and
            %em untyped
            versions.
          %h2#concepts Concepts
          %h3#abstraction_and_application Abstraction and application
          %p
            The basic constructs of lambda calculus are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+abstractions"} lambda abstractions
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em applications
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is an expression of some sort involving a free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            (possibly vacuously), then the lambda abstraction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x. t
          %p
            is intended to represent the function which takes one input and returns the result of substituting that input for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. (x+1))
            is the function which adds one to its argument. Lambda expressions are often convenient, even outside lambda calculus proper, for referring to a function without giving it a name.
          %p
            Application is how we “undo” abstraction, by applying a function to an argument. The application of the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to the argument
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is generally denoted simply by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(t)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f)t
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f t)
            all denote the same thing as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f t
          %p
            Application is generally considered to associate to the left. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mi v
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u v w
            denotes the application of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi y
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. (\lambda y. x+y))
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x+y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x y. x+y)
          %h3#evaluation_and_reduction Evaluation and Reduction
          %p
            %em Evaluation
            or
            %em reduction
            is the process of “computing” the “value” of a lambda term. The most basic operation is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+reduction"} beta reduction
            and consists in taking a lambda abstraction at its word about what it is supposed to do when applied to an input. For instance, the application
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. x+1) 3
            reduces to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3+1
            (and thereby, presuming appropriate rules for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.t)(u)
            is defined as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution#avoiding+variable+capture"} capture-avoiding substitution
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.t)(u) \to_\beta t[u/x].
          %p
            Terms which can be connected by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag"} zigzag
            of beta reductions (in either direction) are said to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em beta-equivalent
          %p
            Another basic operation often assumed in the lambda calculus is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+reduction"}> eta reduction
                \/expansion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with the lambda abstraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. f x)
            which does nothing other than apply
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to its argument. (It is called “reduction” or “expansion” depending on which “direction” it goes in, from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. f x)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            or vice versa.)
          %p
            A more basic operation than either of these, which is often not even mentioned at all, is
            = succeed ";" do
              %em alpha equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi y
                    %mo .
                    %mi f
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. f x) \to (\lambda y. f y)
          %p
            More complicated systems that build on the lambda calculus, such as various
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
          %p
            In good situations, lambda calculus reduction is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confluent+category"} confluent
            (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Church-Rosser+theorem"} Church-Rosser theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+form"} normal form
            %em normalizing
            if its reduction is also terminating—more precisely,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is said to be
            %em weakly-normalizing
            if there exists a finite sequence of reductions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi …
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi n
                  %mo ↛
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \to t_1 \to t_2 \to \dots \to t_n \nrightarrow
          %p
            terminating in a normal form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_n
            %em strongly-normalizing
            if
            %em all
            sequences of reductions lead to a (by confluence, necessarily unique) normal form. In general, there exist terms which do not normalize by any reduction sequence, although simply-typed lambda calculus and many other typed variants of lambda calculus are strongly-normalizing.
          %h2#variants Variants
          %h3#pure_lambda_calculus Pure lambda calculus
          %p
            In the untyped (or “pure”) lambda calculus, every term can be applied to any other term. As an example of the sort of freedom this allows, we can form the term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = \lambda x. x x
            which applies its argument to itself, and then define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = u(u)
          %p
            as the self-application of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mi x
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ω
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mi ω
                  %msub
                    %mo →
                    %mi β
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \to_\beta x x[u/x] = u(u) = \omega \to_\beta \omega \to_\beta \dots
          %p thus giving the classic example of a non-terminating program.
          %p
            In pure untyped lambda calculus, we can define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            using the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Church+numerals"} Church numerals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is represented by the operation of
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi λ
                    %mi f
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 = \lambda f. (\lambda x.f (f x))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            as input and returns a function that applies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            twice. Similarly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi f
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 = \lambda f. (\lambda x.f x)
            is the identity on functions, while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi λ
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = \lambda f. (\lambda x . x)
            takes any function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to the identity function (the 0th iterate of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p
            The most natural sort of
            %em model
            of pure lambda calculus is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+object"} reflexive object
            in a cartesian closed category, that is, an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            together with a pair of maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %munder
                              %mo ⇄
                              %mi ℓ
                            %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi U
                            %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{U &amp; \overset{a}{\underset{\ell}{\rightleftarrows}} &amp; U^U}
          %p
            representing
            = succeed "pplication" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "ambda," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \circ \ell = 1
            (to validate the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            equation). Such data thereby witnesses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^U
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is that every term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon U^U
            have a fixed-point; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed-point+combinator"} fixed-point combinator
          %p
            From the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            and in particular the “types à la Church” perspective, such a reflexive object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            can also be seen as the intrinsic type of
            %em every
            lambda term, and so the untyped lambda calculus is sometimes referred to (a bit cheekily) as really being “uni-typed”. On the other hand, from the “types à la Curry” perspective, it is also possible to begin with the pure lambda calculus as a foundation, and then try to ascribe individual terms more precise types. For example, the normalizing terms of pure lambda calculus can be characterized precisely as those which are typable in an
            %span.newWikiWord
              intersection type
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/intersection+type"} ?
            system.
          %h3#simply_typed_lambda_calculus Simply typed lambda calculus
          %p
            In
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simply typed
            lambda calculus, each variable and term has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f t
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is of some type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B = B^A
            whose domain is
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f t
            is then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a variable of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is a term of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            involving
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x. t
            has type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
          %p
            Without some further type and term constructors, there is not much that can be done, but if we add a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            (that is, a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            with constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\to N
            %em all
            computable functions, although we can go beyond
            = succeed "s;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primitive+recursive+function"} primitive recursive function
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ackermann+function"} Ackermann function
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+recursive+function"} partial recursive function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixpoint"} fixpoint
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinator"} combinator
          %p
            Simply typed lambda calculus is the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+categories"} cartesian closed categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p Every cartesian closed category gives rise to a simply typed lambda calculus whose basic types are its objects, and whose basic terms are its morphisms, while
            %li
              %p Every simply typed lambda calculus “generates” a cartesian closed category whose objects are its types and whose morphisms are its equivalence classes of terms.
          %p These two operations are adjoint in an appropriate sense.
          %h3#functional_programming Functional programming
          %p
            Most
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming"} functional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming languages
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinator"}> combinator
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatory+logic"} combinatory logic
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed-point+combinator"} fixed-point combinator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+lambda-calculus"} quantum lambda-calculus
          %h2#references References
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+Roger+Hindley"}> J. Roger Hindley
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+P.+Seldin"} Jonathan P. Seldin
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lambda-Calculus and Combinators – An Introduction
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511809835"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511809835
          %p
            Introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simply typed
            = succeed "-calculus:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          %ul
            %li#Girard89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"} Jean-Yves Girard
              (translated and with appendiced by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"} Paul Taylor
              and
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yves+Lafont"} Yves Lafont
              = succeed "," do
                %em Proofs and Types
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => ""} ISBN:978-0-521-37181-0
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/stable/Proofs+Types.html"} webpage
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.paultaylor.eu/stable/prot.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The idea that untyped lambda calculus can be modeled internally to a cartesian closed category as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+object"} reflexive object
            (an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            such that the exponential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^U
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+Scott"} Dana Scott
            in
          %ul
            %li
              Dana Scott. Relating theories of the
              = succeed "-calculus." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              %em To H.B. Curry: Essays on Combinatory Logic, Lambda-Calculus and Formalism
              (eds. Hindley and Seldin), Academic Press, 403–450, 1980.
          %p This followed his earlier work constructing an explicit such model in the category of domains:
          %ul
            %li#Scott70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+S.+Scott"}> Dana S. Scott
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Outline of a mathematical theory of computation
                %em Fourth Annual Princeton Conference on Information Sciences and Systems
                (1970) 169–176. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://ropas.snu.ac.kr/~kwang/520/readings/sco70.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Scott-TheoryOfComputation.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Scott76
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+Scott"}> Dana Scott
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Data types as lattices
                %strong 5
                3 (1976) 522–587 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1137/0205037"}> doi:10.1137/0205037
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/files/3287/PRG05.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Another, more recent take on pure lambda calculus as a certain kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            (called a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+theory"}> lambda theory
            ”) can be found in
          %ul
            %li#Hyland13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"}> Martin Hyland
              \.
              %em Classical lambda calculus in modern dress
              Mathematical Structures in Computer Science, 27(5) (2017) 762-781. doi:
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129515000377"}> 10.1017/S0960129515000377
              \.
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.5762"} arxiv:1211.5762
        .revisedby
